I haven't seen the movie, I'm afraid, but it's a nifty idea all right.
If I were implementing a golf bag in SLASH'EM, I'd ignore the weight 
burden thing, and give it the following properties:

- When unidentified, describe as a "leather bag" (like a medical kit). 
When identified, a "golf bag".

- Tourists would have this identified automatically, and be given a small
chance of starting with one.

- Allow it to hold long swords, katana, two-handed swords, rifles, 
shotguns, clubs, and staves, but not polearms, sabres, short swords, 
machine guns, or rocket launchers. Randomly generated golf bags 
should have a chance of including a golf club* inside, but not other
items.

- Not lockable, can fit into other containers, prevents water damage, 
price of 50, weight of 20.

- (a)pplying a golf bag once per turn does not use any time, like a
stethoscope. Using it more than that takes up a turn.

- If cursed, gives fumbling. "You trip over your bag! You make a lot 
of noise!"

- If blessed, automatically alerts you to rust traps on visible
squares. "Water trap ahead!" If hallucinating, "Blue skies ahead!"

*Golf clubs don't exist, of course. I'd make it a club with +2 to hit,
and 1-4 damage vs. all monsters. Descriptive would be a "thin club" when 
unidentified. Tourists would automatically have this identified. I would 
make it rustable, with a weight of 15.

Tourists starting with a golf bag should have a small chance of also
starting with a golf club.

a)pplying a golf club to an item on the ground would behave like 
kicking the item, but with no chance of leg damage, and a message, 
"fore!". If hitting a boulder or loadstone, or if the club is cursed, 
"thump!". If hallucinating, "three!" or "whackety!", respectively.

If you -really- want to mix it up, an artifact golf club would make
a neat tourist gift. Aside from a small hit and damage bonus, if
wielded and not cursed during ascension/escape, would prompt - after 
all the other endgame prompts, "Cheat on your score (y/n)?" If yes, 
gives a small (5%?) bonus to the score.

